IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cc-offences`
IMPORT `cc-qa`

-- The Criminal Code (WA) -- cases for the Q&A layer (cc-qa.l4): every exported question, for every role and every
-- situation. Generated by the scratchpad script qa/b13/gen_cc_cases.py; the expected values were written by hand from
-- the deposited text and are in that script.
--
-- Each question is asked for the role it is not about, with every other input an ASSUMEd value that has none: the answer
-- must be `does not arise for you`, and reading any of those inputs would stop the evaluation with an error ("it is an
-- assumed term"). Each question with the situation input is asked, for its own role, with each value of `what the
-- offence is about` under which it does not arise, every later input ASSUMEd, and must answer `does not arise for you`.
-- Where the situation holds, each question is asked on facts worked by hand, and once against the baseline export's
-- own answer on the same facts (for the new questions, on the baseline's fixture for the other side).
--
-- One limit (graffiti finding 31): the baseline reads `My part in it` and `What it threatened` through OTHERWISE, so
-- for those an ASSUMEd value that is read would not be caught. The situation is read only through the layer's four
-- helpers, which list every value.
--
-- Facts are the baseline's (cc-cases-offences.l4): k021, Jake at 15 with the store's goods; k063, Jake at 29 with the
-- paint and glue at his door, on the assumed reading of the paint.
--
-- BASELINE. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Cases -- the Q&A layer`

ASSUME `an unasked fact` IS A BOOLEAN
ASSUME `an unasked number` IS A NUMBER
ASSUME `an unasked date` IS A DATE
ASSUME `an unasked offence` IS A `What the offence is about`
ASSUME `an unasked part` IS A `My part in it`
ASSUME `an unasked thing done` IS A `What I did with the thing`
ASSUME `an unasked way of stealing` IS A `How I stole it`
ASSUME `an unasked set of circumstances` IS A `The circumstances`
ASSUME `an unasked threat` IS A `The threat made to me`
ASSUME `an unasked threatened thing` IS A `What it threatened`
ASSUME `an unasked damage` IS A `The damage done to my property`

`as the accused` MEANS `I am accused of an offence, or did the act`
`as the victim` MEANS `an offence was committed against me or my property`

`stealing` MEANS `a thing taken or kept (stealing)`
`a threat made` MEANS `a threat`
`damage done` MEANS `damage to property`



`Jake's phone case and earbuds, 20 November 2021` MEANS `What I did with the thing` WITH
    `the thing belongs to someone` IS TRUE
    `the thing is inanimate and movable, or has been made movable` IS TRUE
    `the thing is an animal s 370 makes capable of being stolen` IS FALSE
    `I took it` IS TRUE
    `I converted it to my own or someone else's use` IS FALSE
    `I meant to keep it from its owner for good` IS TRUE
    `I meant to keep someone's special property in it from them for good` IS FALSE
    `I meant to use it as a pledge or security` IS FALSE
    `I meant to part with it on a condition about its return I might not meet` IS FALSE
    `I meant to deal with it so it could not be returned in the same condition` IS FALSE
    `it is money and I meant to use it as I liked, even meaning to repay it` IS FALSE
    `a court finds it fraudulent on other grounds` IS FALSE
    `it had been lost and I found it` IS FALSE
    `I knew who the owner was` IS FALSE
    `I believed on reasonable grounds the owner could not be found` IS FALSE
    `I moved it or physically dealt with it` IS TRUE
    `I acted on an honest claim of right without intent to defraud` IS FALSE

